<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Auto Backup rules for devices on API < 31.
   See https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/data/autobackup

   The 10-day blockade ledger lives in app-private files (filesDir/blockade.json). Including it here
   means it is uploaded to the user's Google cloud backup and restored on reinstall, giving the
   blockade best-effort uninstall survival without any storage permission.

   The 鍛錬 training history lives in exercise.db. Only the main database file is included, never the
   -wal or -shm siblings: backing up a WAL without its exact matching main file restores an
   inconsistent pair. The backup agent copies the file while the app is not running, and an unclean
   WAL is recovered on the next open, so the main file alone is the safe, standard choice.

   These are include-lists: anything not named here is NOT restored. 鍛錬 keeps its state in two
   DataStore files besides the database, and both must be named or a restore brings a year of training
   back with every setting silently reset —

     · tempo_gym.preferences_pb — the 鍛錬 preferences: 振動 / 音 / 音声, 支度の長さ, both rest defaults,
       単位, 画面を消さない and safetyNoteAcknowledged. A restored install that re-asks the safety note
       and re-arms sounds the user switched off is a worse first impression than a fresh one.
     · gym_store.preferences_pb — the durable history-lost flag. It travels with the database on
       purpose: restoring a corrupt-flagged history without its flag would render the loss as
       記録はありません, which is the one thing §7.1 forbids.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="file" path="blockade.json"/>
    <include domain="file" path="datastore/tempo_settings.preferences_pb"/>
    <include domain="file" path="datastore/tempo_gym.preferences_pb"/>
    <include domain="file" path="datastore/gym_store.preferences_pb"/>
    <include domain="database" path="exercise.db"/>
</full-backup-content>
